I reviewed `AccessInfo` and consolidated its duplicated bit-test idiom.

## What I changed (`jadx-core/.../jadx/core/dex/info/AccessInfo.java`)

The class already has a single-source bit-test helper:
```java
public boolean containsFlag(int flag) {
    return (accFlags & flag) != 0;
}
```
…yet ~18 `isX()` accessors each re-inlined that exact expression (`(accFlags & AccessFlags.X) != 0`), and `makeString` had three more raw masks. I routed them all through the helper:

```java
public boolean isPublic()  { return containsFlag(AccessFlags.PUBLIC); }
public boolean isStatic()  { return containsFlag(AccessFlags.STATIC); }
public boolean isSynchronized() {
    return containsFlag(AccessFlags.SYNCHRONIZED | AccessFlags.DECLARED_SYNCHRONIZED);
}
// ...etc, plus STRICT/SUPER → containsFlag(...), ENUM → isEnum() in makeString
```

## Why it's safe
`containsFlag(X)` *is* `(accFlags & X) != 0`, so every rewrite is byte-for-byte equivalent — including the combined-mask case, since `containsFlag(a | b)` tests "any of these bits set," matching the original `(accFlags & (a | b)) != 0`.

I deliberately left three things untouched:
- **`isPackagePrivate`** — it's `(accFlags & VISIBILITY_FLAGS) == 0` (combined mask, `== 0` semantics), not a single-flag "contains" test, and `VISIBILITY_FLAGS` isn't an `AccessFlags` magic constant (would clash with `containsFlag`'s `@MagicConstant` annotation).
- The `VISIBILITY_FLAGS` manipulations (`changeVisibility`, `getVisibility`, `isVisibilityWeakerThan`) and the bit-clear in `remove` — not membership tests.

Result: the single-flag membership tests now have one source of truth; net change is 22 lines rewritten, no behavior change. Braces balanced; verified by inspection (module isn't buildable here without network/cached deps).

