Done. I reviewed `jadx-core`'s utility layer and refactored `Utils.java` with two safe, behavior-preserving changes.

## What I changed (`jadx-core/.../jadx/core/utils/Utils.java`)

**1. Deduplicated `collectionMap`** — its body was a byte-for-byte copy of `ListUtils.map` (same `jadx.core.utils` package). It now simply delegates:
```java
public static <T, R> List<R> collectionMap(Collection<T> list, Function<T, R> mapFunc) {
    return ListUtils.map(list, mapFunc);
}
```
Both guard `null`/empty identically, so this is exactly equivalent. The 22 callers are untouched.

**2. Single source of truth for the primitive descriptor table** — `smaliNameToJavaName` (`V`→`void`…) and `javaNameToSmaliName` (`void`→`V`…) each hand-wrote the *same* nine-entry correspondence as a switch, in opposite directions — two places to keep in sync. I replaced both switches with one declared map whose inverse is derived programmatically:
```java
Map<Character, String> toJava = new HashMap<>();
toJava.put('V', "void"); ... toJava.put('D', "double");
SMALI_PRIMITIVE_TO_JAVA = Collections.unmodifiableMap(toJava);
Map<String, String> toSmali = new HashMap<>(toJava.size());
toJava.forEach((smali, java) -> toSmali.put(java, String.valueOf(smali)));
JAVA_PRIMITIVE_TO_SMALI = Collections.unmodifiableMap(toSmali);
```
The methods now handle their special cases (`L`/`[`/array-suffix) explicitly and look up the rest. The two directions can no longer drift apart.

## Verification
- Logic equivalence checked case-by-case for every input class (empty, primitives, `L`-objects, `[`-arrays, unknowns) — all preserved.
- These two name methods have only one caller outside `Utils` (a GUI fallback parser, `MethodDeclaration`), not the hot decompile path, so swapping a `switch` for a map lookup has no meaningful perf impact.
- Braces balanced; net −46 lines. No imports became unused (`ArrayList`/`HashMap`/`Collections` all still used elsewhere).
- The project is **not buildable in this environment** (no network/cached Gradle deps), so I verified by inspection rather than compilation — consistent with how this repo has been handled before.

